      "claim": "Sorbitol, another hexitol, shows F=0.36-0.38 in shunted patients and 0.27-0.29 in cirrhotic patients; the zero values in normal volunteers are attributed to complete hepatic first-pass removal, not poor absorption, and are not transferable to ascorbic acid.",
      "card_ids": ["C07", "C28", "C37", "C59", "C44", "C57", "C65"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Glucose, a polar monosaccharide, shows F=0.722-0.843 in healthy/T1DM humans, 38% in children, and 70-100% in OGTT estimates; L2 adds high values in birds and ponies, supporting high F for actively absorbed polar sugars.",
      "card_ids": ["C16", "C24", "C25", "C54", "C33", "C51", "C55"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Propylene glycol, a small polar diol, has very high/close-to-complete oral bioavailability, supporting that small polar molecules can exceed 20%.",
      "card_ids": ["C22", "C46", "C47"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Gemcitabine's low oral bioavailability (10%, poor) is attributed to first-pass metabolism by cytidine deaminase; ascorbic acid lacks this liability, so this low-F context is not transferable.",
      "card_ids": ["C01", "C05", "C13", "C19", "C21", "C27", "C29", "C30", "C34", "C41", "C53", "C68"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Additional contexts (topiramate >80%, frusemide ~40%, gallium maltolate 25-57%, pipemidic acid 93%, diflunisal 89.5%) support F>=20% but have lower structural transferability.",
      "card_ids": ["C17", "C09", "C03", "C08", "C14", "C23", "C42", "C49", "C61", "C62", "C70", "C31", "C18"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The physicochemical prior's unfavorable passive permeability is uncertain; its own caveat notes active transport (SVCT1/2) could significantly enhance ascorbic acid absorption, and the empirical pattern of high F in polar small molecules supports F>=20%.",
      "card_ids": ["C04", "C07", "C16", "C22"]
    }
  ],
