      "card_ids": ["C44", "C57", "C65", "C34", "C41", "C53", "C68", "C58", "C60"]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct absolute oral bioavailability measurement for ascorbic acid is present in the supplied cards; all support is transfer-based.",
    "Ascorbic acid absorption is dose-dependent via SVCT1/2; the threshold F>=20% may not hold at very high gram doses, but typical oral doses are not specified.",
    "Sorbitol zero-F normal records show first-pass can dominate for polyols; relevance to ascorbic acid is uncertain without direct hepatic extraction data.",
    "Some context cards have canonical SMILES mismatching the named drugs (frusemide, pipemidic acid, diflunisal), reducing transferability.",
    "No formulation, dose, or species match for ascorbic acid is provided."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "Prediction high: L1 and L2 evidence from structurally related small polar polyhydroxy compounds (mannitol, sorbitol in shunted/cirrhotic, glucose, propylene glycol) shows oral bioavailability at or above 20%, and the low-F records (sorbitol in normal subjects, gemcitabine) are explained by first-pass metabolism/clearance mechanisms not applicable to ascorbic acid, which has active transport. The physicochemical prior is uncertain and does not outweigh this empirical pattern."
}

